SII_translation:
Hail Prosperity! In the 5th year of king Rājakesarivarman, on the day of the solar eclipse occurring in the month of Kaṉṉi,

[Viraco]ḻan of Viḷattūr nāṭu made a gift of land made pure by pouring water, to last as long as the moon and the sun endure, to the parameśvara of Tiruppaḻuvūr, providing for the sacred food offering (tiruvamirtu) at three sessions (potu), with each session consisting of half a nāḻi, totaling six nāḻis for the three sessions, plus one piṭi of ghee per session amounting to three piṭis, two portions of curry per session amounting to six portions of curry, one nāḻi of curd per session amounting to three nāḻis, five betel nuts per day amounting to fifteen betel nuts daily, along with sandalwood, camphor, and one sacred perpetual lamp (nontāviḷakku). The land granted for all these to endure as long as the sun and moon last is one veli of land.

The boundaries of this land granted in Tiruppaḻuvūr are:
To the east, ... and six māvu;
To the right of the Taṇṭuṟai side up to one māvu, and to the west of the Marutampāṭi seven māvu on the Kiḻarceri side;
To the south, north of the neṭṭṟu māvu on the Taṇṭuṟai side;
To the west, east of the muṭakku aru māvu on the Kiḻarceri side and three māvu of Vaṇṇāraccey;
To the north, south of the porocañcey on the Meṟṟeri side and the porocañcey on the Taṇṭuṟai side.

The land enclosed within these four great boundaries is half and three-kāṇi, [excluding what is specified].
To the east of the three-kāṇi on the old temple (tevatāṉam) and Kiḻarceri side:
To the west of the three-kāṇi;
To the south, north of the muṭakku one-māvu;
To the west, east of the southern bank three-māvu;
To the north, south of the kuṉṟaṅcey.
Thus, the land enclosed within these specified four great boundaries is three-kāṇi.

To the east of the mā-kāṇi of Aṟukañcey:
To the west of this temple (tevatāṉam);
To the south, north of the muṭakku aru-māvu on the Kiḻarceri side;
To the west, east of the three-māvu on the Taṇṭuṟai side;
To the north, south of the three-māvu of the aṟavaṭiyārkaḷ.
The land enclosed within these boundaries is one mā-kāṇi.

To the east of the one-māvu of Ṉāvitavaṉ:
To the west of the southern bank three-māvu;
To the south, north of the three-kāṇi on the Kiḻarceri side;
To the west, east of the ... tuṭavai;
To the north, south of the one-māvu on the Kiḻarceri side.
The land enclosed within these boundaries is one māvu.

To the east of the mā-kāṇi of the Kiḷiyūr water-channel:
To the west of the bank;
To the south, north of the old Kiḷiyūr water-channel;
To the north, south of the uncultivated land water-channel (puṟampulattu vāykkāl).
The land enclosed within these boundaries is one mā-kāṇi.

To the east of the mā-kāṇi on the southern mouth of the neṭṭṟu māvu on the Taṇṭuṟai side:
To the west of this;
To the south, north of the two māvu on the Kiḻarceri side and one māvu on the Meṟṟeri side;
To the west, east of the half-māvu on the Kiḻarceri side;
To the north, south of the neṭṭṟu māvu.
The land enclosed within these four great boundaries is one mā-kāṇi.

To the east of the three-kāṇi of this water-channel:
To the west of the water-channel;
To the south, north of the one-māvu on the Kiḻarceri side;
To the west, east of the old temple (tevatāṉam);
To the north, south of this old temple (tevatāṉam).
The land enclosed within these four great boundaries is three-kāṇi.

To the east of the mā-kāṇi-araikkāṇi at the head of the river:
To the west of the round one-māvu on the Taṇṭuṟai side;
To the south, north of the quarter-cey at the head of the river;
To the west, [mutalai paḻiili...]
...
To the north, south of the mā-kāṇi on the Taṇṭuṟai side.
The land enclosed within these boundaries is one mā-kāṇi araikkāṇi.

[Lines 65–86 contain damaged or fragmentary boundary descriptions involving various fractions of māvu, including measurements of one māvu, half-māvu, kāṇi, muntirikai, muṭakku one-māvu, nānocci, eṭṭu-māvu, and kāṇittuṭavai.]

The total land enclosed within these fourteen boundaries is ... veli of land.

... [this land] was given as a tevatāṉam gift free of obligations by me, Mahāmālaya Irukkuveḷāṉ, also called Parāntakan Viracoḻan.
This is under the protection of all great devotees (panmāhēśvara rakṣai).

Hail Prosperity!

Hail Prosperity! In the 8th year of king Rājakesarivarman, the assembly of Tiruppaḻuvūr in Viḷattūr nāṭu, having gathered as the village assembly, sold land to Cāttaṉ Āccaṉ of Ātavattūr in Uṟaiyūr kūṟṟam after taking the agreed purchase price, in order to provide for ceremonial bathing (snapam) for the paramēśvara of Tiruppaḻuvūr at every saṅkrānti. Having received the price, they engraved and gave [this stone inscription] for the dry land.

The boundaries of the dry land are:
To the east, west of the path leading to the river;
To the south, north of the dry land on the Kiḻarceri side and west of this, and south of the dry land on the Meṟṟeri side;
To the west, east of the common village water-channel;
To the north, south of the dry land on the Meṟṟeri side.

The land enclosed within these specified four great boundaries, amounting to seven māvu, selling the entire land without any omissions, we, the assembly of Tiruppaḻuvūr, have sold and delivered it.

We shall pay and clear all forms of taxes (iṟai) and forced labor (veṭṭi) arising from this land. It is stipulated that the red-water veṭṭi (cennir veṭṭi) shall be... and the water shall be allowed to flow... To endure as long as the moon and the sun last, in this manner we sold and delivered it, we the assembly of Tiruppaḻuvūr.

Having purchased this from the villagers for this paramēśvara to endure as long as the moon and the sun last... to cause... to be made, I engraved and gave this document (kalli vacakki kuṭutteṉ cāt...).

Hail Prosperity! In the 6th year of king Rājakesarivarman, in this year, for the paramēśvara of Tiruppaḻuvūr in Viḷattūr nāṭu, for the two performers (aṭikaḷmār) who sing the tiruppatiyam at the three sandhis, the land given by Ciṟiyāṉ Parāntakaṉ of Kāraikkuṭi in Viḷattūr nāṭu is the field named Ciṟiyāṉ-vayakkal in Vaṭapaṉaṅkāṭṭu.

The boundaries of this land are:
To the east, west of the earthen boundary;
To the south, north of the northern earthen boundary of Tāḻi-vayakkal;
To the west, east of the path leading to the burial ground (piṇampoku-vaḻi);
To the north, south of the earthen boundary of this cey.

The land enclosed within these specified boundaries, amounting to ten māvu, selling the entire land without any omissions, I, Ciṟiyāṉ Parāntakaṉ of Kāraikkuṭi, have made a gift of land made pure by pouring water, to the paramēśvara of Tiruppaḻuvūr for the two performers who sing the tiruppatiyam at the three sandhis, so that it may endure as long as...

Hail Prosperity! In the ... year of king Rājakesarivarman, in this year, for the paramēśvara of Tiruppaḻuvūr in Viḷattūr nāṭu, Mātaṉ Aṟiñcikai of Tiruveḷḷaṟai engraved on stone and gave the raised land (meṭum) enclosed within the following boundaries as an endowment (nivantam) for those who worship this paramēśvara, [constituting] the old tevatāṉam of ... mātaṉ ...-yakkal.

The boundaries of this are:
To the east, ... of the red earth;
To the south, north of the water-channel flowing eastward;
To the west, east of the eastern flower garden (nantavānam) of the sacred temple (śrī koyil);
To the north, south of the main road.

The land enclosed within these boundaries, including the raised ground, I, Mātaṉ Aṟiñcikai, have engraved on stone and given.

Hail Prosperity! In the 10th year of king Rājakesarivarman, for the paramēśvara of Tiruppaḻuvūr in Viḷattūr nāṭu, I, Irāmatevaṉ of Kāraikkuṭi in this nāṭu, gave as kārāṇkiḻamai the wet land (nirnilam) in Vaṭapaḻaṅkāṭṭu, amounting to 3 portions of land given to this god.

The boundaries are:
To the east, west of the water-channel;
To the south, north of my dry land (puñcey);
To the west, ... up to one māvu;
To the north, ...

The land enclosed within these specified boundaries, amounting to ...

Hail Prosperity! In the 6th year of king Rājakesarivarman, for the paramēśvara of Paḻuvūr in Viḷattūr nāṭu, ... so that it may endure as long as the moon and the sun last, ...

The boundaries are:
To the east, ... Aṭṭu ...;
To the south, ...;
To the west, ...;
To the north, south of the peri-...

The land enclosed within these boundaries ... this paramēśvara ...

Hail Prosperity! In the third year of Kopparakesarivarman, the land granted to endure as long as the moon and the sun last by Araṅkaṉ Veṇṇāvaleṉ of Kāraikkuṭi, also known as Kāraikkuṭāṉ, for those who perform the daily worship (tiruvārā-tiṉai) of the Parameśvarar of Tiruppaḻuvūr:

The boundaries of the land are:
To the north, south of the paddy field of Pāḷūr;
To the east, west of the stone boundary;
To the south, east of the ditch of Maṇalorumāvarai;
To the north of the palmyra field (paḷicey);
To the east of the three-quarters of a kāl of land (nilimukkāṇi);
To the east of the southern bank there;
To the east of the devadānam there;
... contained within these bounds, without loss...
... exempt from taxes (iṟai) and forced burdens (eccoru), and free from veṭṭi and vetiṉai...
... the god there shall have this engraved on stone and take possession of it.

In this manner, I, Araṅka Veṇṇāvaleṉ of Kāraikkuṭi, gave this as a devadānam gift to the yogiyār. This is under the protection of the great Maheśvaras (paṉmāheśvara-rakṣai).

Hail Prosperity! In the 6th year of king Rājakesarivarman, for the paramēśvara of Paḻuvūr in Viḷattūr nāṭu, to the person who strings flowers for the sacred flower bed (tiruṭpaḷḷittāmam), two māvu of land from the old tevatā1nam was engraved on stone and given.

The boundaries of this are:
To the east, west of the land of those who perform worship;
To the south, north of the water-channel flowing ...;
To the west, east of this same water-channel;
To the north, south of the Pāḷūr-vayakkal.

The land enclosed within these specified boundaries, amounting to two māvu, I, Ūṟaṉ Araṅkaṉ, have engraved on stone and given.

This is under the protection of all great devotees (panmāhēśvara rakṣai).

Hail Prosperity! In the 4th year of king Rājakesarivarman, we, the assembly of Poykaikuṭi, a brahmadēyam in Vaṭakarait-Tiruvāli nāṭu...

To the tiruveḷḷaṭaiāṇṭār of ...vūr, our village assembly [gave] as the old tevatānam of our village half and two māvu, and fertile lands...

For the pātti-nilam:
To the east, west of the land of Āttriraiyan Cuvaran ṟāmotiran;
To the south, ... of Kāccuvan...;
To the west, east of the land of Āttrirain Cuvara Nakkan;
To the north, south of the land of the assembly members...
The land enclosed within these... is 30... māvu.

Furthermore, for the 4 māvu in the western division (melkoṭṭakam) of this village:
To the east, west of the small upper channel (uṭciṟukāl);
To the south, north of the Kurukūr water-channel;
To the west, east of the land of Kāccuvan Nampan Kāṭan;
To the north, south of the land of ... and the land of ...
The land enclosed within these specified four boundaries is 4 māvu.

And for the land in ... the boundaries are:
To the east, west of the kolai-nilam of Kurukāvūr;
To the south, north of the land of Kāccuvan Nama Nakkan and younger brothers... and the land of Vattan Kuṇatira Nārāyaṇa;
To the west, ... and east of the land of Ātrayan Cuvara Nakka;
To the north, south of the boundary of Kurukāvūr.
Thus, the land amounting to half and two māvu...

The manner in which we remitted all taxes (iṟai) for this land to endure as long as the sun and moon last is: we, the assembly of Poykaikuṭi, having received cash through the appropriate persons... Aṇukkap Pallavaraiyan, have remitted the taxes and given it.

We, the assembly of Poykaikuṭi, have engraved and given this, along with the flowing water-channel, as a protection for all great devotees (pālmāheśvara rakṣai). This is the writing of the Ācārya Mallaṭi...

Hail Prosperity! In the fifteenth year of Kopparakesarivarman, for the lord of Tirukkurukāvūr Tiruveḷḷaṭai... ninety perpetual sheep (cāvā-muvā-perāṭu) given for one perpetual lamp. From these ninety sheep, ninety nāḻis of ghee arriving annually, without fail, for this lamp to burn as long as the moon and the sun last.

The lamps to be maintained are:
Two lamps, which Ārūrannaṭikaḷ of Kurukāvūr, the manṟāṭi, is bound to maintain;
One lamp, which Nāraṇa Kuṇacilan, the manṟāṭi of Kurukāvūr, is bound to maintain;
One lamp, which... Koṟṟan, known as Paṭalai, of Kurukāvūr is bound to maintain;
One lamp, which the manṟāṭi Kaṭaiyan Peruvaḻi of Kurukāvūr is bound to maintain;
...
One lamp, which... is bound to maintain;
One lamp, which Nāraṇan Iṅkamayan, the manṟāṭi of Kurukāvūr, is bound to maintain.

Hail Prosperity! In the fifteenth year of Kopparakesarivarman... in the brahmadēyam of Kaṭalvāyil... the land of the brāhmaṇī Īśvaraṉ, belonging to the Pāratāyan lineage of landholders; the land of the brāhmaṇī Koṟṟi, daughter of Ṇilakaṇṭaṉ, belonging to the Āttiraiyaṉ lineage; [and] to Somattiyaṉ Appiyāṭṭaṉ, the landholder of Kaṭalvāyil... [granted by] Ūrkiḻān Pāvaṉittan Nāraṇan Kantaṉ...

The boundaries of the land:
To the west...
To the south, north of the inner small channel (uḷ-ciṟu-vāykkāl);
To the west, east of the boundary channel (ellai-vāykkāl);
To the north, south of Govinda's channel (govintavāykkāl).
All the land encompassed within these four stated major boundaries, and beyond this, for the half māvu, the boundaries are:
To the east, west of the land of Kāppiyan Kāḷi Kaḻiyan and his younger brothers;
To the south, north of the land of Cāttaṉ Nāraṇan of the sacred feet (tiruvaṭikaḷ);
To the west, east of the land of the Lord of Tiruṟṟaḷi (tirumeṟṟaḷi-devar);
To the north, south of... channel and the land of the Lord of Tiruṟṟaḷi.
All the half māvu of land encompassed within these four stated major boundaries.

For the three māvu in Kiḻūr, the boundaries are:
To the east, west of the land of Ūr-kiḻān Pāvaṉittan Kāṭampālai;
To the south, north of the Kuṇṟappoḻan channel;
To the west, the channel of Moci Nākaṉ...
To the south of the land of Ūr-kiḻān Pāvaṉittan Kāṭan Kecuvaṉ.
All the three māvu of land encompassed within these four stated major boundaries.

For the two mākkāṇi in Koṭṭakam, the boundaries are:
To the east, west of the land of Kāśyapan Ceruvan Nampaṉ;
To the south, north of the Irumpili channel;
To the west, Ūr-kiḻān...
To the south, north of the land of Ūr-kiḻān Pāvanittaṉ Tavaraṉ Centaṉ.
All the two mākkāṇi of land encompassed within these four stated major boundaries.

For the half kāl in Iñce, the boundaries are:
To the east, west of the land of Ūr-kiḻān Pāvanittaṉ Nārāyaṇan Irāmatevaṉ;
To the south, north of the land of Irātitaran Nārāyaṇan Kumaraṉ;
To the west... east of...
To the south, north of the land of Ūr-kiḻān Pāvanittaṉ Kuṇatiranā[ra]ṇaṉ.
All the half kāl of land encompassed within these four stated major boundaries.

Beyond this, for one māvu, the boundaries are:
To the east, west of the land of Pālāciriyan Tattan Cantiracekaraṉ;
To the south, north of the land of Pāvanittan Kāṭampālai;
To the west...
To the south of the Irumpili channel.
All the one māvu of land encompassed within these four stated major boundaries.

Total land: one and seven mākkāṇi.

For the house site (illa-maṉai), the boundaries are:
To the east, west of the land of Pāvanittan Vaikuntaṉ and his younger brothers;
To the south, north of the land of Pāvanittan Kāṭan Kecuvaṉ;
To the west... east of... deva's land;
To the south, north of the land of Mokiliyan Niṉṟān Vaṭukan.
All the house site encompassed within these four stated major boundaries, situated in Śrī-Paḻuvanmahādevī-caturvvētimangalam... west of the kāvati... south of the Mūlapperuṭai channel...

[The record continues with details of measurement, leveling by rod (kayvantavāṟṟāl), and the assignment of one and a half māvu of land, situated at the northern end of the three māvu granted to the members of the square (caturattār) including Āttakūr Ātitta Piṭārakramavittaṉ].

Hail Prosperity! In the 2nd year of king Rājakesarivarman, the assembly of Nāṅkūr [gave this to] the god of Tiruveṇkāṭu.

Ayyan Vempaṭikaḷ among the Pārttivacekara-teriñca-kaikkoḷar gave sixty kaḻañcus of refined gold (cempon) to provide for one kalam of superior food (uttamāgram) for the Lord's food offerings.

The land given by the assembly after taking this gold and selling it in the southern hamlet (teṉpiṭākai) is bounded by:
To the east, east of the land sold by the assembly;
To the south, south of the Tiruveṇkāṭu water-channel;
To the west, west of the uttamāgram land of Kottappic Coḻar;
To the north, south of the Paṭṭaṉeru-vaḻi.

The land lying in the middle is one veli.

We, the assembly of Nāṅkūr, shall protect and deliver this land exempt from all taxes (iṟai) and forced burdens (eccoru).

This is to endure as long as the moon and the sun last.

Hail Prosperity! In the 7th year of king Rājakesarivarman, the boundaries of the land sold by us to Kaṅka ṉampalavaṉ Kaṇṭarātitta-coḻa Viḻupparaiyaṉ, who is the Kuvaḷālamuṭaiyāṉ of Kaṅkarasāyiram in Ulamakātevipuram in Oymānāṭu:

To the east, west of the golden Kaṇṭarātitta embankment located amidst our mañcikkam called Kaṇṭarātitta-nilam;
To the south, north of the Veṇkāṭu major embankment;
To the west, east of the Kalikaṇṭaka major embankment;
To the north, south of the ... below the tank.

Within these four boundaries... the gold taken for various endowments... including the sword and the tank... the sacred inner shrine (tiru-uṇṇāḻikai)... as a sacred flower garden (tirunantavānam) land kāṇi... [the total measurements and sales details up to the māvu and kuḻi levels, involving a price measured with the rod (kol) totaling 4,583...], sold outright with absolute title to ... palava... Kaṇṭarātittaṉ by the merchants (nakarattom), in the manner we possess this land, in Ulamakamātevipuram at the sacred Kayi...
